; ONTOLOGY — verisectorium-plan-specific decisions
; Vanilla udon. Provisional throughout; this instance is the last ad-hoc one and is
; deliberately trialing tradeoffs — decisions here are *its* law, not verisectorium law.
; When a decision proves out (or fails), that datum routes to the plan segments (ch. 7/11).

|ontology[verisectorium-plan] :date <2026-08-05> :status provisional
  :governs plan/OUTLINE.md and plan/last-adhoc-src/

  |summary
    What our outline columns mean, where identity lives, what we call our segments,
    and what shape a segment is expected to take. Eventually parts of this become schema.

  |outline[columns] :of OUTLINE.md :order determinative
    :assembly-column Tag
    |column[state]
      First column so it reads as the row's current condition at a glance.
      Independent resettable flag, not a rung on a ladder. Initial vocabulary:
      proposed · drafted · checked. Expect additions (citations-check, prose-check, ...)
      that reset when the segment is edited. Empty on gap rows.
      `proposed` means: this row is a first iteration of putting the topic on the
      outline — the row itself is still up for revision — not a definitive segment
      merely awaiting its draft.
    |column[expected-type]
      What we currently *guess* the segment's type will be, assigned at outline time,
      before drafting. A prediction, deliberately kept once the real type is known,
      so prediction-vs-actual is cheap data about outline-time foresight.
    |column[type]
      The actual type, filled at drafting time from the segment's own declaration.
      Blank until then. Divergence from expected-type is information, not an error.
    |column[tag]
      The slug, as a wikilink: [[slug]]. This is the identity column and the
      assembly manifest — build/assembly walks this column in outline order.
      Filename in last-adhoc-src/ equals the slug. No numbers, no type prefixes
      (a trialed divergence from asf's role-prefix convention — gathering data).
      A literal --GAP-- here (with the other columns empty) marks a gap row.
    |column[summary]
      One-line summary of the segment. Called Summary, not Claim, until we know
      what the ontology of segment kinds actually is here.

  |gap-semantics
    A --GAP-- row means: we do not know how many segments this region will
    eventually be, but the Summary names the main topic or thing to establish.
    Gaps carry no identity — nothing may depend on or cite a gap. If a single
    next segment is genuinely known well enough to name, give it a real row.

  |segment[expected-shape] :status sketchy
    :dir last-adhoc-src/
    :one-topic-per-file true
    What we call them: **segments** (generic), atoms when speaking of the pattern.
    Expected types so far (guesses, see expected-type column):
    |type[def]    A definition — establishes vocabulary, carries no truth claim
    |type[form]   A formulation — a claim about how or why something works
    |type[emp]    An empirical claim — readable, claim-first, grounded by evidence it
                  cites (usually an appendix obs/survey); a chapter-level type
    |type[norm]   A decision about how we work here; not truth-apt; overturn expressly
    |type[obs]    An observation — raw empirical material: specimens, counts, method;
                  appendix-grade — grounds emp/form claims, is cited by them
    |type[survey] A comparative inventory across instances; appendix-grade like obs
    |type[disc]   Discussion / orientation — no warrant of its own
    Format: not yet fixed. Expected sections: a one-line summary, a body stating
    present truth at honest strength, and **Working Notes** (expected on nearly
    every segment — forward residue, not history-dump). Epistemic marking style
    (section vs frontmatter vs inline) is deliberately open — see the ch. 1 gap.
    Frontmatter carries `depends:` — the list of slugs this segment **genuinely
    stands on** (definitional imports, logical antecedents, grounding specimens),
    never mere mentions or see-alsos (those stay wikilinks in prose). Declare
    `depends: []` explicitly when a segment is a root — silence and emptiness
    are different facts. Forward deps on proposed-but-undrafted rows are allowed
    (the row is the identity); a dep on a slug with no row anywhere is an error.
    The atoms form a DAG; outline order may diverge from it deliberately
    ([[appendix-placement]]) but the divergence must be *checkable*, which is
    what the declaration is for.

  |segment[readable-claims-appendix-evidence] :status provisional
    Earned 2026-08-05 from steward review of the first obs batch ("almost impossible
    for me to understand and I *lived* them"). Chapter-level segments are **readable
    claims** — emp or form, stating what is true in plain language a front-to-back
    reader can follow, per the respectful-pedagogy discipline (mental model first).
    Raw material (obs/survey: specimens, counts, method detail) lives in the
    **Appendices part** of the outline and is cited by the claims it grounds — even
    though it is upstream in the dependency DAG. See [[appendix-placement]] for the
    principle and [[identities-over-locations]] + [[provenance-rot-specimen]] as the
    worked exemplar pair (readable claim in chapter · full specimen in appendix).
    Test for the split being right: the claim is understandable without the
    appendix; the appendix carries the verification load without the claim.
    Placement is an **outline decision only** — a segment never says which part
    of the outline it is in (no "Appendix:" titles, no "(appendix)" in cites);
    asf's practice, adopted: appendix-ness lives in the view's chapter heading,
    segments keep ordinary content titles. (Corrected 2026-08-05 after the first
    exemplar violated this.)

  |segment[influx-not-warrant] :status provisional
    Earned 2026-08-05 from first drafting practice (obs/survey batch + same-day
    rewrite after feedback). **plan/INFLUX/ is intake, not a citation store.**
    Segment bodies state evidence directly (what was measured, on what date, by
    what method) or cite **live** sources (paths that remain the source of truth
    after influx is archived). Do not lean on influx filenames or informal
    labels for those files ("field report", "generalization note",
    "logical-model letter") as if they were durable warrants.
    If unintegrated influx still sits behind a segment: put a Working Notes line
    that names the influx path, marks it do-not-cite-as-warrant, and states that
    this segment replaces those pointers. Goal: when INFLUX is fully
    .integrated/ or .archive/, every segment body still stands.

  |segment[parallel-batch-law] :status provisional
    Earned 2026-08-05 from the five-way integration batch (two near-collisions on
    new-slug creation; deps born "illegal" awaiting single-writer row placement).
    - **Slug creation is the unprotected concurrency surface** — [[write-safety]]'s
      hazard in the assembly namespace. During a parallel batch: check the outline
      AND `ls last-adhoc-src/` immediately before Write; where possible, chunk
      assignments by *claim/chapter* (chapter claims are never chunk-local) and let
      appendix specimens stay chunk-local (they are measurements of your own
      material). The lead reserves new slugs on request.
    - **Dep-validity is evaluated at merge, not at draft time.** A depends: entry
      naming a slug whose row is still in a drafter's proposed-rows report is
      work-in-flight, not an error; the checker's mid-batch output must be read
      accordingly. The error condition is a dep on a slug that no file, row, or
      pending report anywhere names.
    - **An appendix segment names its subject, never its consumers** — the citation
      direction lives in the citing claims and in the outline rows. (Multi-consumer
      appendices otherwise force a placement-is-outline-only violation.)

  |segment[readable-claims-tripwire] :status provisional
    The readable-claims norm was violated three more times the day it was minted,
    by an agent that had read it — names don't fire; tripwires do. Mechanical
    trigger, added 2026-08-05: **any table, and any number derived from counting
    more than two things, is appendix-grade** — if one appears while drafting a
    chapter claim, split the specimen out then and there.

  |segment[provenance-registers] :status provisional
    Three registers, not two (the third earned by the tribunal chunk):
    1. **first-hand** — measured/read by this segment's author, dated;
    2. **live-source** — cited to a path that remains authoritative (with the
       caution that live trees move: the honest anchor for a moving tree is the
       live path plus the retained .archive copy);
    3. **inherited at a stated remove** — taken from a document that itself marks
       its verification registers (e.g. sweep-verified vs training-recall); the
       inheriting segment preserves those markers and never lets "a sweep verified
       this" silently become "this is verified." This distinction is exactly what
       erodes under turnover; state the remove.

  |segment[integrated-means-remainder-registered] :status provisional
    .integrated/ has no partial state, so the dispatch rule is: a file may move to
    .integrated only when its landed content has a segment home AND its unlanded
    remainder is registered in a residue file routed by outline row. "Integrated"
    asserts the *pair*, not full absorption. (Earned from the orientation-gate
    note: claim landed, five design points owed — filing it integrated would have
    been true of the claim and false of the note.)

  |cross-references
    [[slug]] everywhere — outline, segments, sidecars. No paths in references;
    the wikilink resolves by basename. Path-carrying provenance is known to rot
    (see [[provenance-rot-specimen]]). Live *source* paths in Method sections
    are allowed when the warrant is an external live tree (asf, vivarium, …)
    — that is different from citing influx copies.

  |sidecars
    INFLUX/ holds gathered-but-unprocessed material (the 2026-08-05 notes gather).
    .integrated/ and .archive/ receive processed influx per the usual estate
    metabolism. PRACTICA / CHANGELOG / HANDOFF live at the verisectorium root
    (one level up) and follow NORMS.md basename conventions.

  |working-notes
    - Whether expected-type/type prediction-tracking earns its cost: revisit after
      the first ten drafts.
    - Column vocabulary for state is a first guess; let real work grow it.
    - This file is .un (transitional per NORMS.md) — becomes proper schema-bearing
      udon when the schema machinery exists.
